  "worked_solution": "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}-{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}})|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
